/**
\defgroup basis_tensor Tensor product bases
\ingroup basis
\brief Tensor products of univariate bases with partial derivatives, integrals, Kronecker Gram matrices, penalties, and transforms.

Header `<laplace/basis/tensor.hpp>` defines \ref laplace::basis::tensor_product_basis "tensor_product_basis", the concept \ref laplace::basis::multivariate_basis "multivariate_basis", and host and device utilities in namespace `laplace::basis`. A `tensor_product_basis` is a lightweight struct copied by value into Kokkos kernels; its evaluation members are marked `LAPLACE_HD` for host and device execution, `cross_gram()` and most free functions run on the host, and \ref laplace::basis::fill_tensor_basis_rows "fill_tensor_basis_rows()" launches a Kokkos kernel. Across four mixed-margin reference cases evaluated at 400 bits, rows stay within \f$11.0\epsilon\f$, expansions within \f$3.00\epsilon\f$, and cross Gram matrices within \f$3.26\epsilon\f$ of their scales.

## Mathematical definition

A tensor product combines \f$D \ge 2\f$ univariate margins indexed by \f$j = 1, \dots, D\f$, fixed at compile time. Margin \f$j\f$ may be any `univariate_basis` (B-spline, cyclic B-spline, Fourier, or Legendre) with \f$K_j\f$ basis functions \f$b_{j,0}, \dots, b_{j,K_j - 1}\f$ and row width \f$w_j\f$ given by `row_width()`. The type `tensor_product_basis<B_1, ..., B_D>` deduces its template parameters from `tensor_product_basis(b_1, ..., b_D)`.

The product basis has \f$K = \prod_{j=1}^D K_j\f$ functions (`dimension()`). For marginal column indices \f$i_j \in \{0, \dots, K_j - 1\}\f$, flat column \f$c = \sum_{j=1}^D s_j i_j\f$ with strides \f$s_D = 1\f$ and \f$s_j = s_{j+1} K_{j+1}\f$ defines the product function
\f[
B_c(x) = b_{1,i_1}(x_1)\, b_{2,i_2}(x_2) \cdots b_{D,i_D}(x_D)
\f]
at \f$x = (x_1, \dots, x_D)\f$. Because the last margin varies fastest, the full vector of product functions is \f$b_1(x_1) \otimes \dots \otimes b_D(x_D)\f$, and a coefficient vector \f$\beta\f$ of length \f$K\f$ is laid out flat in this column order.

Partial derivatives and integrals are selected at run time by an `int` multi-index \f$k = (k_1, \dots, k_D)\f$. Each entry \f$k_j \in \{0, \dots, 6\}\f$ gives the derivative order in \f$x_j\f$, while \f$k_j = -1\f$ selects the definite integral from \f$lower_j\f$ to \f$x_j\f$, where \f$lower_j\f$ is the lower domain boundary of margin \f$j\f$. This defines the mixed partial \f$\partial^k B_c(x)\f$ and the linear expansion \f$\sum_{c=0}^{K-1} \beta_c\, \partial^k B_c(x)\f$.

Each `tensor_product_basis` holds copies of the margins' structs, while their memory stays with their owners. Pass `owner.device()` margins when calling device kernels and `owner.host()` margins when calling host functions. The member template `margin<I>()` returns margin \f$I + 1\f$, where template parameter \f$I\f$ counts from 0, and `for_each_margin()` applies a functor to every margin in order.

The concept \ref laplace::basis::multivariate_basis "multivariate_basis" captures this kernel interface: `margin_count`, `dimension()`, `row_width(k)`, `scratch_size(k)`, `row()`, `row_partial()`, `expansion()`, `expansion_partial()`, and `column()`. A tensor product does not satisfy `univariate_basis`, because its active columns do not form a single contiguous window.

## Domain and special values

The tensor product defines no outside-domain rule of its own. Outside its domain, each non-periodic margin applies its configured rule as described on \ref basis "the basis group", whereas Fourier and cyclic B-spline margins are periodic.

If any coordinate \f$x_j\f$ is NaN, or if a margin uses `outside_domain_rule::nan` outside its domain, every value of the row becomes quiet NaN. Cross Gram matrices and operator penalties integrate over the domain box \f$\prod_{j=1}^D [lower_j, upper_j]\f$, taking one period for each periodic margin.

## Method

### Rows, expansions, and parallel evaluation

Evaluating a row at \f$x\f$ writes the \f$D\f$ marginal first-column indices \f$f_1, \dots, f_D\f$ into `first` and \f$W = \prod_{j=1}^D w_j\f$ values into `out`. Those values form the Kronecker product of the marginal rows with the last margin varying fastest. Entry \f$q\f$ (\f$0 \le q < W\f$) corresponding to offsets \f$(a_1, \dots, a_D)\f$ with \f$0 \le a_j < w_j\f$ belongs to flat column
\f[
\sum_{j=1}^D s_j \big((f_j + a_j) \bmod K_j\big),
\f]
where the modulus affects only cyclic margins. Member `column(k, first, q)` computes this column index on demand for multi-index `k`, and `column(first, q)` handles \f$k = 0\f$; column indices are never stored.

Every kernel call requires a caller scratch buffer of `scratch_size(k)` values, equal to \f$\sum_{j=1}^D w_j(k_j)\f$, where \f$w_j(k_j)\f$ is the active width of margin \f$j\f$ for order \f$k_j\f$. The call writes the marginal rows sequentially into `scratch` and then forms their product in `out`. For two cubic B-spline margins at \f$k = 0\f$, the scratch size is \f$8\f$ and the row width is \f$W = 16\f$.

An integrated margin (\f$k_j = -1\f$) contributes all \f$K_j\f$ integrals \f$\int_{lower_j}^{x_j} b_{j,i}(t)\,\mathrm{d}t\f$ with first column \f$0\f$ and width \f$K_j\f$. A B-spline column ending before \f$x_j\f$ still has a nonzero integral, so keeping only the window of \f$d + 1\f$ values of a B-spline margin of degree \f$d\f$ would drop nonzero entries of the product. Consequently, `row_width(k)` and `scratch_size(k)` use \f$w_j(k_j) = K_j\f$ when \f$k_j = -1\f$ and \f$w_j(k_j) = w_j\f$ otherwise. Parameterless `row_width()` and `scratch_size()` correspond to \f$k = 0\f$. When every \f$k_j = -1\f$, the row gives the integral of each \f$B_c\f$ over the box \f$\prod_{j=1}^D [lower_j, x_j]\f$.

Member `row_partial(x, k, first, out, scratch)` computes the row of \f$\partial^k B_c(x)\f$ and returns its width, and `row(x, first, out, scratch)` evaluates \f$k = 0\f$. Member `expansion_partial(x, k, beta, scratch)` returns \f$\sum_{c=0}^{K-1} \beta_c\, \partial^k B_c(x)\f$ by contracting the marginal rows in `scratch` against \f$\beta\f$ without forming the product row. Member `expansion(x, beta, scratch)` is the case \f$k = 0\f$.

The launcher \ref laplace::basis::fill_tensor_basis_rows "fill_tensor_basis_rows(b, k, x, first, values)" evaluates rows of multi-index `k` (a `std::array<int, D>`) across all \f$n\f$ points of `x` on `x`'s execution space. View `x` has \f$n\f$ rows and \f$D\f$ columns, `first` has \f$n\f$ rows and \f$D\f$ columns, and `values` has \f$n\f$ rows and `row_width(k)` columns. View `values` must be a `Kokkos::LayoutRight` View of `double`, enforced by `static_assert`. The margins in `b` must be accessible from that execution space, and the function allocates an internal scratch View of \f$n\f$ by `scratch_size(k)` doubles.

### Gram matrices and penalties

All Gram matrix and penalty functions run on the host. Given multi-indices \f$k\f$ and \f$l\f$ with entries in \f$\{0, \dots, 6\}\f$, `cross_gram(k, l, out)` writes the dense \f$K \times K\f$ column-major matrix
\f[
G_{kl} = G^{(1)}_{k_1 l_1} \otimes \dots \otimes G^{(D)}_{k_D l_D}
\f]
into a caller buffer of \f$K^2\f$ values, where \f$G^{(j)}_{k_j l_j}\f$ comes from margin \f$j\f$'s `cross_gram()`. Any order outside \f$\{0, \dots, 6\}\f$ throws `std::invalid_argument`. The public function \ref laplace::basis::kronecker_product "kronecker_product(factors, out)", which `cross_gram()` uses, computes \f$F_1 \otimes \dots \otimes F_n\f$ in column-major order of size \f$(\prod \mathrm{rows}) \times (\prod \mathrm{cols})\f$ from a `std::vector<kronecker_factor>`. Each \ref laplace::basis::kronecker_factor "kronecker_factor" describes a dense column-major matrix via `data`, `rows`, and `cols`.

For a constant-coefficient differential operator \f$L = \sum_a c_a \partial^{k^{(a)}}\f$, each term is a \ref laplace::basis::tensor_operator_term "tensor_operator_term" with `coefficient` \f$c_a\f$ and `order` \f$k^{(a)}\f$. The function \ref laplace::basis::tensor_operator_penalty "tensor_operator_penalty(basis, terms, out)" writes the dense \f$K \times K\f$ column-major penalty matrix
\f[
S_L = \sum_{a,b} c_a c_b\, G_{k^{(a)} k^{(b)}},
\f]
which represents \f$\int (L f)^2\f$ over the box in the coefficients of \f$f = \sum_c \beta_c B_c\f$. A sum of squares of several operators is accumulated by summing separate calls. For example, the two-dimensional thin plate penalty sums three calls with single terms \f$\partial^{(2,0)}\f$, \f$\sqrt{2}\,\partial^{(1,1)}\f$, and \f$\partial^{(0,2)}\f$ to obtain \f$G_{(2,0)(2,0)} + 2 G_{(1,1)(1,1)} + G_{(0,2)(0,2)}\f$. Passing all three terms in one call computes the penalty of their sum instead. The function throws `std::invalid_argument` if any `order` does not have \f$D\f$ entries, has an entry outside \f$\{0, \dots, 6\}\f$, or exceeds the degree of a polynomial margin (B-spline or Legendre), where the resulting matrix would not match \f$L\f$.

For a univariate \ref laplace::basis::differential_operator "differential_operator" `op` (see \ref basis_operator "the differential operators page") acting on one coordinate, \ref laplace::basis::marginal_operator_penalty "marginal_operator_penalty(basis, j, op, out)" writes
\f[
G^{(1)}_{00} \otimes \dots \otimes S_j \otimes \dots \otimes G^{(D)}_{00},
\f]
representing \f$\int (L_j f)^2\f$ over the box. Its argument `j` counts from 0 (selecting margin \f$j + 1\f$ in this page's numbering), \f$S_j\f$ is the `operator_penalty()` of `op` on that margin, and the remaining margins contribute their Gram matrices \f$G^{(m)}_{00}\f$, \f$m \ne j + 1\f$. Passing `j` outside \f$\{0, \dots, D - 1\}\f$ throws `std::invalid_argument`, and the limits of `operator_penalty()` on that margin apply.

### Kronecker transforms

On the host, \ref laplace::basis::kronecker_transform "kronecker_transform(t)" accepts a `std::vector<basis_transform>` containing one transform \f$T_j\f$ of size \f$K_j \times q_j\f$ per margin (\ref basis_transform "the basis transforms page"). It returns the dense \f$K \times \prod_{j=1}^D q_j\f$ transform \f$T = T_1 \otimes \dots \otimes T_D\f$, constructed with `basis_transform_from_matrix()`, so the returned object declares no property. Marginal properties nevertheless carry over factor by factor: if every \f$T_j\f$ was built by `make_basis_transform()` with \f$G^{(j)}_{00}\f$ and a penalty \f$S_j\f$, then \f$T^\top G_{00} T = I\f$ and \f$T\f$ diagonalizes every penalty produced by `marginal_operator_penalty()`. It throws `std::invalid_argument` for fewer than two transforms or an empty transform.

Also on the host, \ref laplace::basis::compose_basis_transforms "compose_basis_transforms(a, b)" returns the product \f$T_a T_b\f$, applying `a` (\f$K \times q\f$) first and `b` (\f$q \times p\f$) to the coefficients of `a`, again with no declared property. It throws `std::invalid_argument` when the columns of `a` differ from the rows of `b`, allowing product-level constraints such as a zero-integral constraint to be applied after marginal transforms.

## Accuracy

See \ref accuracy "How accuracy is measured" for error definitions and reference conventions. Reference values are computed directly from the definitions of the margins without calling the tensor product code: B-spline and cyclic B-spline margins use exact rational arithmetic, Legendre margins use integer coefficients, Fourier margins use mpmath, and all products are formed at 400 bits.

Four cases are tested: a cubic by quadratic B-spline product, a B-spline by cyclic B-spline product with \f$x_2\f$ spanning several periods, a Legendre by Fourier product, and a three-margin product of B-spline, cyclic B-spline, and Legendre bases. Evaluation points include knots and values one ulp on either side, and multi-indices include integrals. Errors are reported in units of \f$\epsilon = 2^{-52}\f$ times a scale: the largest reference value of the row for rows, \f$\sum_c \lvert\beta_c B_c\rvert\f$ for expansions, and the largest entry for Gram matrices and penalties. Both gcc/OpenMP and CUDA (RTX 4070 SUPER) give the same maxima except where noted, with the test bounds:

| Case | Rows | Expansions | Gram | Test bounds (rows, expansions, Gram) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| B-spline by B-spline | 2.31 | 1.17 | 1.74 | 3.5, 1.8, 2.6 |
| B-spline by cyclic B-spline | 11.0 | 3.00 | 3.26 | 16.5, 4.5, 4.9 |
| Legendre by Fourier | 2.10 | 1.70 (CUDA 1.42) | 1.24 | 3.2, 2.6, 1.9 |
| B-spline, cyclic B-spline, Legendre | 4.01 | 1.14 (CUDA 1.35) | 2.67 | 6.0, 2.1, 4.0 |

In the first case, the thin plate penalty is within \f$0.88\epsilon\f$ of its largest entry (bound \f$1.3\f$). Rows of the cyclic case carry the cyclic margin's own error (\ref basis_cyclic_bspline "the cyclic B-spline page"). The tests also verify that every row equals the product of the marginal rows bit for bit (including outside the domain), that NaN coordinates and `outside_domain_rule::nan` make the row NaN, and that `expansion_partial()` matches the scattered row dot product. With `op` equal to \f$D^2\f$ on two margins, `marginal_operator_penalty()` equals `cross_gram()` with both multi-indices \f$(2, 0)\f$ bit for bit. Kronecker products of two Demmler-Reinsch transforms satisfy \f$T^\top G T - I\f$ within \f$7\epsilon\f$ and penalty off-diagonals within \f$2.2\epsilon\f$ of the largest entry. Composing with a zero-integral constraint keeps \f$\lvert C T\rvert\f$ within \f$7.1\epsilon\f$ of \f$\max\lvert C\rvert\f$. Finally, `fill_tensor_basis_rows()` on the device is verified against the host. See `docs/numerics.md`.

## Speed

For two cubic B-spline margins with \f$K_j = 20\f$ functions at \f$10^5\f$ points on CUDA (RTX 4070 SUPER), sparse row evaluation takes \f$0.15\f$ ms and the expansion takes \f$0.093\f$ ms. A sparse row requires \f$136\f$ bytes, compared with \f$3,200\f$ bytes for the same row stored dense.

## Example

The following example builds a product of two identical cubic B-spline margins on \f$[0, 1]^2\f$ and evaluates its expansion at \f$n = 1000\f$ points inside a Kokkos kernel:

\code{.cpp}
#include <laplace/basis/bspline.hpp>
#include <laplace/basis/tensor.hpp>
#include <Kokkos_Core.hpp>

void example_tensor_expansion() {
  const auto owner = laplace::basis::bspline_basis_owner::uniform_clamped(0.0, 1.0, 10, 3);
  const auto m = owner.device();
  const laplace::basis::tensor_product_basis tb(m, m);

  constexpr int n = 1000;
  Kokkos::View<double**> x("x", n, 2);
  Kokkos::View<double*> beta("beta", 100);
  Kokkos::View<double*> eta("eta", n);

  Kokkos::parallel_for("eval_tensor_expansion", n, KOKKOS_LAMBDA(const int i) {
    double xi[2] = {x(i, 0), x(i, 1)};
    double scratch[8];  // tb.scratch_size() is 8 for two cubic margins
    eta(i) = tb.expansion(xi, beta.data(), scratch);
  });
}
\endcode

## Module types and functions

All public types, concepts, and functions are defined in namespace `laplace::basis`:

- \ref laplace::basis::multivariate_basis "multivariate_basis" — Concept for multivariate kernel bases
- \ref laplace::basis::tensor_product_basis "tensor_product_basis" — Tensor product of \f$D \ge 2\f$ univariate bases
- \ref laplace::basis::kronecker_factor "kronecker_factor" — Column-major matrix factor descriptor for Kronecker products
- \ref laplace::basis::kronecker_product "kronecker_product()" — Column-major Kronecker product of dense matrices
- \ref laplace::basis::tensor_operator_term "tensor_operator_term" — Weighted partial derivative term in a tensor differential operator
- \ref laplace::basis::tensor_operator_penalty "tensor_operator_penalty()" — Penalty matrix of a constant-coefficient tensor differential operator
- \ref laplace::basis::marginal_operator_penalty "marginal_operator_penalty()" — Penalty matrix of a univariate operator acting along one margin
- \ref laplace::basis::kronecker_transform "kronecker_transform()" — Kronecker product of per-margin coefficient transforms
- \ref laplace::basis::compose_basis_transforms "compose_basis_transforms()" — Product of two coefficient transforms
- \ref laplace::basis::fill_tensor_basis_rows "fill_tensor_basis_rows()" — Parallel kernel evaluating tensor basis rows over a View of points

## References

- F. Johansson and others, mpmath: a Python library for arbitrary-precision floating-point arithmetic, version 1.3.0, https://mpmath.org/.
*/
